      <h1>{frontmatter.title}</h1>
      <time>{frontmatter.date}</time>

      <p class="intro">Calico vs Cascadia comes down to one question. Do you want a tile puzzle that forgives a bad placement, or one that charges you for it until the last turn?</p>

      <p>If you want the answer with no scrolling, here it is. Buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Cascadia+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Cascadia</a> if you have never played a tile-laying game. It runs about $37, teaches in 10 minutes, and shows you every option before you commit. Buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Calico+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Calico</a> if you already love this kind of spatial puzzle and want one that squeezes harder. It runs about $42.</p>

      <p>Both come from Flatout Games and both were illustrated by Beth Sobel, so the spec sheet cannot separate them. Both play 1 to 4 players in 30 to 45 minutes. I own both, and Cascadia has hit my table more than 40 times against about 12 for Calico. That gap is not about which game is better, and it is the thing most comparisons skip.</p>

      <h2>What a Tile Puzzle Actually Asks of You</h2>
      <p>Here is the key insight. Every tile-laying game is two games stacked on top of each other. One is the spatial puzzle in front of you. The other is the draft, the shared market you pull tiles from. Games feel tight or loose depending on which of those two you can control, and Calico and Cascadia hand you very different amounts of control over both.</p>

      <p>Start with the puzzle. In Calico you hold two patch tiles in hand and place one into an empty space on your quilt board. Each patch has a color and a pattern. Colors earn buttons, patterns attract cats, and three Design Goal tiles along the border check the six patches around them at the end of the game. Every placement is permanent. There is no rearranging, ever.</p>

      <p>In Cascadia you take one of four face-up pairs, a habitat tile and a wildlife token, and add the tile to your landscape. Matching terrain is not required to place a tile. It only matters when corridors score at the end. Then you drop the wildlife token onto any empty tile showing that species, which can be a tile you placed five turns ago.</p>

      <p>That one rule is the whole personality of the game. The worst habitat tile in the Cascadia box can still be buried somewhere cheap. The worst patch in Calico has to live in your quilt until the game ends.</p>

      <p>Now the draft. Cascadia shows four pairs at all times. Nothing is hidden, and a Nature Token can even let you split a pair and take the tile and animal you actually want. Calico shows you three patch tiles in the market, and when you take one, the refill comes blind from the bag. You can see your next two options, and after that you are guessing with everyone else.</p>

      <p>But here is where the two games split again. The market is where both get their interaction. Somebody takes the pair you needed, and now three turns of planning are dead. Notice that neither game lets you touch another player's board. Cascadia's ending and Calico's ending are both personal, quiet, and a little lonely. The fight happens over tiles, not over territory.</p>

      <h2>Two Numbers That Decide It For You</h2>
      <p>Pay attention to this number: zero. That is how many empty spaces you have left at the end of Calico. Your quilt fills completely, so the last few turns are not about scoring anything new. They are about fitting whatever is left into the gap you saved. When I finish a Calico game I feel like I solved something. Whether I solved it well is a separate question.</p>

      <p>The Cascadia number is 20. You take exactly 20 turns in the base game, then the habitat tiles run out. Your map is never finished. It just stops. My first five games of Cascadia all ended with mountains I never connected and a salmon card I ignored completely.</p>

      <p>Here is why that matters at your table. Materials that run out create a rush, and boards that fill up create dread. If your group plays slow, Cascadia's clock keeps things moving because everyone gets the same 20 turns. If your group likes the satisfaction of a completed build, Calico gives you that finished quilt to look at afterward.</p>

