      <h2>The Difference Nobody Mentions</h2>
      <p>I thought I understood both games after a year of playing them. Then I did the boring thing and wrote down my scores.</p>

      <p class="twist-box"><strong>Here is the contradiction.</strong> Every ranking calls Cascadia the lighter game, and lighter usually means less to think about. My log says the opposite. On my last ten plays of Calico I never felt out of the game until the final third, because I still had spaces left to fill. On three of those ten plays of Cascadia I lost the game in the draft around turn 6, watching a pair I needed go to someone else twice in a row before the first round was over. The lighter game is the one that punished my drafting earlier.</p>

      <p>However, the reason comes down to market size. Cascadia hands you four visible pairs and 20 turns, which means the draft is a real fight you can lose. Calico hides the refill, so early on you cannot punish anyone on purpose. You just take the best of three and hope the bag is kind.</p>

      <p>That is the whole twist. Cascadia is not a soft game with easy decisions. It is a soft game with sharp decisions, and it puts them in the first ten minutes. Calico spreads its pressure across the whole board, and its bite comes later, when you realize the pattern you committed to in turn 4 cannot be finished.</p>

      <h2>When Calico Wins</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Calico+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Calico</a> is a quilt puzzle from designer Kevin Russ, published in 2020 by Flatout Games and AEG. You are competing to sew the coziest quilt. Same-color groups earn you buttons, defined pattern groups attract cats, and the three Design Goal tiles on your board want specific groupings of colors and patterns around them. It won the 2020 American Tabletop Award for Game of the Year, and the art is the reason half the people who own it bought it.</p>

      <p>Where it wins is at the moment a plan lands. In my best game I chained a triple of teal patches into a button while the same three patches completed a cat pattern, and the Design Goal next to them paid out too. Four scoring systems, one placement. Cascadia rarely gives you that, because its wildlife cards only ever ask one question at a time.</p>

      <p>Calico is also the better solo game of the two if you like a strict puzzle. The goal is a score, not a finish line, and my best is 61. The honest flaw, and the reason mine only comes out a few times a year, is that a badly tangled quilt is painful to finish. When I took the wrong pattern in turn 5 of a recent game, I still had 20 minutes of placing tiles with nothing left to win. Calico has no mercy mechanic. The board does not care that you are stuck.</p>

      <h2>When Cascadia Wins</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Cascadia+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Cascadia</a> is a habitat-building game from designer Randy Flynn, published by Flatout Games in 2021. You draft pairs, grow a Pacific Northwest landscape, and score two ways. Habitat corridors reward the largest connected group of each terrain. Wildlife tokens score against whichever cards are active that game, so the hawk wants your birds lined up one way and the bear wants family groups another.</p>

      <p>It won the 2022 Spiel des Jahres, the biggest award in board games, and the 2023 International Gamers Award for best solo game. My wife has never asked to play a board game twice in one night except this one.</p>

      <p>Where it wins is teaching. I have taught Cascadia to six people who had never played a modern board game, and every single one was making real choices by turn 3. The family variant drops the math for younger kids, and the box comes with enough scoring cards that the puzzle changes between plays.</p>

      <p>The honest flaw is the criticism the award reviews all level at it: there is almost no interaction. Nobody attacks you. Two players can sit at the same table, build their own landscapes, and compare scores at the end. If your group needs banter or table talk in a game, Cascadia will feel like four people doing homework next to each other. My group plays it on quiet weeknights and saves the loud games for the weekend.</p>

      <p>If you need five or six players, the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Cascadia+Landmarks+expansion+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Cascadia: Landmarks expansion</a> adds a fifth and sixth player along with new scenario cards. It was about $33 when I checked, which is close to what the base game costs, so only buy it if your table is actually that big.</p>

      <h2>The Third Option: Knitting Circle</h2>
      <p>If the Calico puzzle sounds good but the $42 and the mercy-free scoring do not, look at <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Knitting+Circle+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Knitting Circle</a>. It is a standalone follow-up to Calico from the same studio. You are a knitter laying yarn tiles into patterns instead of a quilter laying patches, and it plays 1 to 4 in the same 30 to 45 minute window.</p>

      <p>It costs $31, which is the cheapest of the three, and it is the easiest to teach to a mixed table. If you already own Calico and want a second box with the same feel for a different group, this is the one I would grab before any expansion.</p>

      <h2>The Shelf Test</h2>
      <p>Here is the Shelf Test. Would this box survive 10 plays on a shelf that already holds 40 games? Plenty of tile puzzles do not. They get one evening of wonder and then become decoration.</p>

      <p>Cascadia passes on replay value alone. The wildlife scoring cards rotate, the scenarios change the goals, and it plays at every player count without falling apart. I am at 40 plays and still seeing setups I have not tried.</p>

      <p>Calico passes for a smaller reason. It survives because of the score. Each game is a number, and a number you can beat is a game you come back to. It is the only game in my collection where a friend texted me a screenshot of a 74 two days after learning it.</p>

      <p>Ten plays is the bar. If only one of these makes it there in your house, I would bet on the one you can teach to a stranger without a rulebook open. That is Cascadia in most homes.</p>

      <p class="cta">Here is the part that matters. For a first tile-laying game, grab <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Cascadia+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Cascadia</a> at about $37. If you already know the genre and want the tighter puzzle, grab <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Calico+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Calico</a> at about $42. Prices bounce, so catch Cascadia under $35 and Calico under $40 and you have done well. Browse the <a href="/games/strategy">full strategy lineup</a> for more of this weight class, or read the <a href="/blog/best-strategy-board-games">best strategy games</a> roundup and the <a href="/blog/best-solo-board-games">best solo games</a> list. If your group is kids and grandparents, the <a href="/blog/best-family-board-games">best family games</a> list is the better start. New to tile puzzles? Try <a href="/blog/how-to-play-azul">how to play Azul</a> first, or compare the other gateway pair in <a href="/blog/splendor-vs-azul">Splendor vs Azul</a>. The <a href="/game/cascadia">Cascadia page</a> and the <a href="/game/calico">Calico page</a> have specs and current prices too.</p>

